  "divide.modeColumn": "By a column",
  "divide.modeColumnHelp": "Use a column of the file, such as province or stratum, and choose who collects the plots of each value.",
  "divide.peopleTitle": "Who collects",
  "divide.orderTitle": "Which plots go together",
  "divide.orderFile": "In the order of the file",
  "divide.orderFileHelp": "Each person gets plots that come one after another in the file.",
  "divide.orderMixed": "Mix them",
  "divide.orderMixedHelp": "Each person gets plots from all over the file.",
  "divide.orderNearby": "Keep plots that are close on the map together",
  "divide.orderNearbyHelp": "Each person gets the plots of one area of the map, and Collect Earth lists their plots in an order that goes from each plot mostly to a nearby one. The plots are lined up along a path that covers the file's area, and each person takes the next stretch of it; in a division by a column, each value's plots follow the same path.\n\nIt needs latitude and longitude: the survey's coordinates must be in latitude and longitude, and each plot needs them in the two columns after the plot id. Plots without them are shared out last, in file order. Copies to compare go to the next person, who mostly holds a neighbouring area. Moving plots by hand afterwards keeps this order.",
  "divide.orderNearbyHelp.short": "Each person gets one area of the map, their plots in an order that moves to nearby ones.",
  "divide.orderNearbyMissing": "{count} plots of this file have no latitude and longitude: they are shared out last, in the order of the file.",
  "divide.orderNearbyMissingOne": "1 plot of this file has no latitude and longitude: it is shared out last, in the order of the file.",
  "divide.orderNearbyUnknown": "Not available: this server does not say whether the plots of this file have a latitude and longitude.",
  "divide.orderNearbyOtherSrs": "Not available: the survey's coordinates are in {srid}, not in latitude and longitude.",
  "divide.orderNearbyNoPlaces": "Not available: no plot of this file has a latitude and longitude (the two columns after the plot id).",
