  "person.hoursExplain": "Plots and checks first submitted in each hour, in your time zone ({zone}).",
  "person.hourColumn": "Hour",
  "person.submissions": "{n} submitted",
  "person.submissionsColumn": "Submitted",
  "person.speedTitle": "Speed (an estimate)",
  "person.speedExplain": "The time between one submission and the next while working, taken from when each plot was first submitted. Submissions 30 minutes or more apart start a new working session, and that gap is left out; the figure above the chart is the median of the gaps that remain. The chart counts the gaps by length, from under 15 seconds (the first column, in the warning colour) to 30 minutes. A warning appears when some gaps are shorter than the project's “very fast” limit, 15 seconds unless a coordinator changes it.",
  "person.speedExplain.short": "The time from one submission to the next while working.",
  "person.speedNone": "Not enough submissions close together yet.",
  "person.speedMedian": "Usually {d} between two submissions (the median of {gaps}, in {sessions}).",
  "person.veryFastOne": "{n} submission came less than {d} after the one before. That is often a plot answered without looking at it.",
  "person.veryFastMany": "{n} submissions came less than {d} after the one before. That is often a plot answered without looking at it.",
  "person.speedEstimate": "This is an estimate from when the plots were submitted, not a measure of the work: a gap also holds the time between plots, and any pause under 30 minutes counts. Use it to see a pace or a run of very fast submissions, not to judge one plot. The time each plot's form was really open, which Collect Earth sends with the plot, is shown to coordinators under “Time spent on each plot” on the person's page.",
  "person.speedEstimate.short": "An estimate from submission times, not the time really spent.",
  "person.gapColumn": "Time to the next submission",
  "person.timesColumn": "Times",
  "person.seconds": "{n} s",
  "person.minutes": "{n} min",
  "person.minutesSeconds": "{m} min {s} s",
  "person.under": "under {d}",
  "person.between": "{from} to {to}",
  "timeSpent.title": "Time spent on each plot",
  "timeSpent.explain": "How long each submitted plot's form was open and in use in Collect Earth, from opening the plot to submitting it, added up over every time it was opened. Pauses of more than 5 minutes are left out. Collect Earth sends this time with each plot; plots sent by an older version have none and are counted apart. Only the first assessments of plots in plot files are counted, not quality checks, and only coordinators see these figures.\n\nThe usual time is the median, and the middle half are the plots between the quickest quarter and the slowest quarter. The chart counts the plots by time, the bands wholly under the quick limit in the warning colour. On the People tab the table gives each person's figures, the largest share of quick plots first; on a person's page their figures are set beside the team's.",
  "timeSpent.explain.short": "How long each submitted plot's form was open and in use in Collect Earth.",
  "timeSpent.none": "No plot has a time yet. Collect Earth sends it with each plot submitted from this version on.",
  "timeSpent.teamMedian": "The team usually takes {d} per plot (the median of {n} plots); the middle half take {from} to {to}.",
  "timeSpent.quickRule": "A plot that took under {d} ({share} % of the team's usual time) is called quick: it may not have been looked at carefully. The limit follows the team: it is worked out again from all the timed plots each time the figures are loaded, so a long form gives a higher limit, and nothing is called quick until 20 plots have a time.\n\nNothing is blocked or changed. Open a quick plot from “Quick plots” (the quickest first) and look at its answers: an easy plot can be quick. When one person has many quick plots, look at several of them before talking to them.",
  "timeSpent.quickRule.short": "A plot that took under {d} is called quick: open it to see.",
  "timeSpent.quickWait": "Plots are called quick once {n} plots have a time (now {timed}).",
  "timeSpent.untimed": "{n} submitted plots have no time: they were sent by an older Collect Earth.",
