  "health.title": "Survey health",
  "health.explain": "How each question works in practice, for the next version of the survey: how often it is left empty, how often it is answered with an option like “other” or “unknown”, how often the checks agree, and how often a decision took the check's answer. A question is worth rewording when the checks agree on under 80 % of its plots, “other” and the like are chosen on 10 % or more, it is left empty on 20 % or more, or decisions often changed it; each figure needs at least 20 plots.\n\nOnly those questions are listed at first, each with its reasons; the button under the table lists every question, and “How these are counted” explains each column. Nothing here changes the data or the survey.",
  "health.explain.short": "How each question works in practice; the questions worth rewording come first.",
  "health.count": "{plots} first assessments of plots; {checked} plots with a quality check, {decided} of them decided for the check.",
  "health.noData": "There are no first assessments yet.",
  "health.none": "Nothing stands out: by these figures no question needs rewording.",
  "health.showAll": "Show all {n} questions",
  "health.showFewer": "Show only the questions worth rewording",
  "health.capped": "Only the first {n} of {total} questions are listed.",
  "health.colQuestion": "Question",
  "health.colReasons": "Worth rewording because",
  "health.colEmpty": "Left empty",
  "health.colOther": "Other, unknown, none or no data",
  "health.colAgreement": "Checks agreeing",
  "health.colChanged": "Changed by decisions",
  "health.noReason": "Nothing stands out.",
  "health.reason.low_agreement": "The checks agree on only {rate} of {n} plots (under {min}).",
  "health.reason.low_agreementOne": "The checks agree on only {rate} of {n} plot (under {min}).",
  "health.reason.often_other": "{share} of the plots answering it choose {answers}.",
  "health.reason.often_empty": "Left empty on {share} of the plots where this is known.",
  "health.reason.often_changed": "A decision took the check's answer, which differs here, on {n} of the {decided} plots decided for the check.",
  "health.share": "{share} ({n} of {known})",
  "health.tooFew": "Too few plots to judge ({n} of {known})",
  "health.notKnown": "{n} do not say",
  "health.counts": "{n} of {known}",
  "health.shareChecks": "{share} ({n} of {known} checks)",
  "health.tooFewChecks": "Too few checks to judge ({n} of {known})",
  "health.changed": "{n} of {decided} plots",
  "health.required": "Required",
  "health.calculated": "Worked out from other answers: reword the questions it comes from.",
  "health.notesTitle": "How these are counted",
  "health.noteEmpty": "Left empty: of the first assessments that say whether the question was left empty, those that left it empty. The server keeps empty answers only since a recent version: older records have nothing for them, so only records that keep them count (those holding an empty answer, and those received since the project's first one). The others “do not say”. Questions inside a group of questions never say. A question shown only for some answers can be empty on purpose: its share is shown, but it is never a reason to reword it. Look at the survey before rewording.",
  "health.noteOther": "Other, unknown, none of the above or no data: the first assessments answering with an option whose label says so in one of the survey's languages (English, Spanish, French or Portuguese), such as “Other (specify)” or “Sin datos”, or, for an option without a label, whose code says so (such as “other” or “99”). Out of the first assessments answering the question; a plot with two such options counts once. Classes such as “Other land” are not counted. “None” alone (such as “None (no disturbance)”) is a real answer: it is shown apart, for information, and is never a reason.",
  "health.noteAgreement": "Checks agreeing: of the plots with a first assessment and a quality check where at least one of them answers the question, those where the two answers agree (numbers within their margin).",
  "health.noteChanged": "Changed by decisions: of the checked plots where a coordinator took the check's answers, those where the check's answer to this question differs from the first assessment's. Numbers within their margin are not counted as changed.",
  "health.noteOrder": "Order: each figure is judged only with at least {min} plots (checks for the agreement). First the questions where the checks agree on under {agreement} of the plots (the lowest first), then those answered with “other” and the like on {other} or more, then those left empty on {empty} or more, then those changed on {changed} or more plots decided for the check and on {changedShare} or more of them. Then the others, in the survey's order.",
  "health.fewRecords": "Not enough records that say what was left empty ({n}; at least {min} are needed)",
  "health.inGroup": "Not known: inside a group of questions, empty answers are not sent",
  "health.conditional": "Shown only for some answers: an empty answer may be expected, so it is never a reason here.",
  "health.colNone": "“None” (an answer of its own)",
  "queue.title": "Review queue",
  "queue.explain": "The plots worth a decision, one at a time: first the checks that differ from the first assessment and have no decision yet, then the plots worth a look, then the plots decided “Both need another look” that were submitted again. Each plot shows the differing answers, why it is worth a look and links to see it in Google Earth or Earth Map.\n\n“First is right” or “Check is right” says which answers the export's “final” column takes; “Both need another look” sends the plot back to everyone who assessed it. When you decide, the next plot comes; “Undo” takes a decision back. The menus narrow the queue to a person or a kind. A reviewer's own plots are left out: someone else decides them.",
  "queue.explain.short": "The plots worth a decision, one at a time; when you decide, the next one comes.",
  "queue.keys": "Keys: 1 First is right · 2 Check is right · 3 Both need another look · J next plot · K previous plot · C write a comment. They work while the queue has the focus (click the plot or reach it with Tab), never while you type; Esc leaves the comment. After a decision the next plot takes the focus, so you can go on with the keys. Holding J or K moves on plot after plot; holding 1, 2, 3 or C acts only once. With Ctrl, Cmd or Alt held, the keys do nothing.",
  "queue.keys.short": "Keys: 1 First is right · 2 Check is right · 3 Both need another look · J next · K previous · C comment",
  "queue.count": "Plots to look at: {n} ({differences} with a check that differs, {flags} worth a look, {resubmitted} submitted again).",
